Plain English Explanation

Think of it like a flashlight on a basketball.

Imagine Earth is a basketball sitting on a table. You are a tiny ant standing on the side of the ball — that's Perris, California.

Now hold a flashlight 12,500 miles directly above the ant. That flashlight is GPS satellite PRN 24. The light it shines spreads out in a cone and hits a big circular patch of the basketball's surface. Everything inside that lit circle can "see" the flashlight. Everything outside that circle is in the dark.

The 76° rule:

That lit circle on the basketball only reaches about 76 degrees of arc in every direction from directly below the satellite. Think of it like this — if the satellite is directly over you, it's directly overhead at 90°. As it moves away, your view angle drops. Once it goes past 76° away from you, it dips below your horizon and you can't see it anymore, same as how the sun disappears at sunset.

The 108° problem:

At 1:06 AM on April 8th, PRN 24 wasn't just a little past the horizon. Its ground position — the point on Earth directly below it — was 108 degrees of arc away from Perris. That's like the satellite being on the completely opposite side of the Earth relative to you. Specifically it was over the southern Indian Ocean near Antarctica's latitude.

108° is much greater than 76°, so PRN 24 was deeply invisible — not even close to the edge of the horizon. There was a solid wall of Earth in the way.

So why did the phone even list it?

Because your phone is smart enough to know PRN 24 exists — the way you might know London exists even if you can't see it from your backyard. The phone downloaded a GPS almanac — essentially a schedule of where every satellite is going to be — either from the cellular network or from other satellites it was already talking to. That almanac told it: "PRN 24 is out there." But knowing something exists and actually receiving its radio signal are two different things entirely.

The "A" flag in GNSSLogger means exactly that: Almanac only. The phone had the schedule but no signal. The blank elevation, blank azimuth, and blank signal strength confirm the receiver searched for PRN 24, found nothing, and correctly reported silence.

