Yes, completely normal — and here is exactly why.

Your phone downloads a cheat sheet in advance.

Every GPS receiver — phone, car nav, dedicated GPS unit — regularly downloads what is called an almanac. Think of it as a TV guide for satellites. It tells the receiver: here are all 32 satellites, here is roughly where each one will be over the next several weeks. This almanac gets pushed to your phone automatically through the cellular network before you ever open a GPS app. It is how your phone can get a location fix in seconds instead of minutes.

The almanac lists ALL 32 satellites — even the ones on the wrong side of Earth.

There is no filter that says "only show me satellites I can currently see." The receiver loads the whole list. When GNSSLogger displays PRN 24 with an "A" flag and blank signal data, it is simply reading from that pre-loaded list and saying: "I know this satellite exists, I searched for its signal, I found nothing." That is working exactly as designed.

It would actually be MORE suspicious if it DIDN'T appear.

If a satellite that should be in the almanac was completely missing from the list, that could indicate a tampered receiver, spoofed almanac data, or a jammed frequency. The fact that PRN 24 shows up listed but silent is the textbook healthy behavior of a properly functioning GPS receiver.

The one-liner:

    A GPS receiver listing a satellite it cannot hear is exactly like your phone showing a WiFi network as "saved" even when you are miles away from that router. It remembers it exists. It just cannot connect right now.

Completely, thoroughly, unambiguously normal.

