Analyzed satellite signal data and orbital mechanics patterns

These two screenshots are from 05:05–05:06, about 4 hours after your earlier captures. Several things stand out immediately.
Image 1 — C/N0 Signal Plot (05:06)

PRN 24 is now in the legend — listed as G:24:L1 in salmon/orange. That is exactly what orbital mechanics predicts. Four hours later, that satellite rotated from the southern Indian Ocean into view over your horizon. Its appearance on schedule is confirmation that the orbital math is correct and the earlier absence was legitimate.

The overall signal picture shows healthy C/N0 values clustering between 25–40 dB-Hz for most satellites, consistent with your earlier Status screenshots.
Image 2 — Spoof/Jam Tab (05:05)

This is the more interesting screen. Break it down:

The black solid line = AGC (Automatic Gain Control) This is your receiver's internal amplifier constantly adjusting itself. It is sitting rock-steady at approximately 34 dB across the entire time window. That flat, stable AGC is actually the single most important jamming indicator in the entire app. A jammer or strong interference source forces the AGC to drop sharply as the receiver cranks its gain trying to compensate. No drop. No spike. Flat line.

The orange dashed line = GPS L5 C/N0 average — sitting around 57–63 dB-Hz. Strong and consistent.

The yellow line = QZSS satellites J1 and J5 — reading around 10 dB-Hz. This is notably weak, but QZSS is a Japanese regional constellation optimized for Asia-Pacific elevation angles. From Perris, California those satellites are near the horizon — low signal is expected.
The Bottom Line on This Pair

The Spoof/Jam screen is essentially saying: no jamming detected, no spoofing indicators, AGC environment is clean.

The only flag worth noting for your documentation is the QZSS weakness, which is geometry-explainable, not interference-explainable.


